---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const inequalities = [
  ["AI", "Final authority"],
  ["Outcome", "Evidence quality"],
  ["Criticism", "Negative outcome"],
  ["Source", "Truth"],
  ["Study count", "Scientific certainty"],
];

const outcomeScale = [
  "Strong Positive",
  "Positive",
  "Mixed / Leaning Positive",
  "Neutral / Inconclusive",
  "Mixed / Leaning Negative",
  "Negative",
  "Strong Negative",
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How WiseEvidence separates outcome, quality, confidence, and criticism — and why classification is not proof of efficacy."
>
  <article class="prose-none max-w-2xl">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">Methodology</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-muted">
      WiseEvidence is built around one commitment: keep the dimensions of evidence
      <em>separate</em>, and never present a tidy score as a scientific verdict.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">The dimensions are independent</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-muted">
      A study's <strong>outcome</strong> (what it reported) is not the same as its
      <strong>evidence quality</strong> (how rigorously it was done). A positive study is not automatically
      high quality; a negative study is not automatically low quality.
      <strong>Criticism</strong> of a study's method is not the same as a negative outcome, and
      <strong>confidence</strong> reflects the whole body of evidence, not any single paper.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <caption class="sr-only">Concepts WiseEvidence keeps distinct</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-300">
            <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink">This</th>
            <th scope="col" class="py-2 pl-4 font-semibold text-ink">is never treated as</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            inequalities.map(([a, b]) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-200">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-ink">{a}</td>
                <td class="py-2 pl-4 text-ink-muted">{b}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Outcome is a spectrum, not a binary</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-muted">
      Findings are represented across a spectrum — supportive, mixed, neutral, inconclusive, and
      critical — never as a positive-versus-negative switch:
    </p>
    <ol class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
      {
        outcomeScale.map((label) => (
          <li class="rounded-full border border-slate-300 px-3 py-1 text-sm text-ink-muted">
            {label}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Classification is not proof of efficacy</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-muted">
      Any internal score exists only to help organize and visualize evidence. It is
      <strong>not</strong> a validated scientific measurement, and a classification on this platform is
      <strong>not</strong> a claim that a treatment works. WiseEvidence describes the state of the research;
      it does not adjudicate efficacy.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">AI assists; humans decide</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-muted">
      AI may extract metadata, summarize, and <em>suggest</em> a study type, outcome, or evidence level.
      Those suggestions are stored separately from human-reviewed values, with their provenance preserved,
      and they never become the final public classification without human review. AI is an assistant,
      not the final authority.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Provenance is preserved</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-muted">
      Every record keeps its source and identifiers. A source is not the same as the truth — it is
      where a claim comes from, kept transparent so it can be checked.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-10 text-sm text-ink-muted">
      This page describes the intended model documented in the project architecture. The
      classification pipeline and research catalogue are implemented in later milestones.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
